   "id": "77d64fcd",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# 🏷️ \"THE PRICE IS RIGHT\" Capstone Project\n",
    "## Day 1: Data Curation & Dataset Engineering\n",
    "\n",
    "Welcome to the Week 6 capstone project! Over the course of this week, we are building **The Price Is Right** — an end-to-end Machine Learning and Generative AI system that predicts the retail price of an Amazon product based on its description, bullet features, and technical specifications.\n",
    "\n",
    "### 📅 The 5-Day Progression\n",
    "1. **DAY 1: Data Curation (Today)** — Ingesting massive web-scraped Amazon data, stripping noise, standardizing units, deduplicating, balancing price and category distributions, and publishing to Hugging Face Hub.\n",
    "2. **DAY 2: Data Pre-processing & Prompt Formatting** — Converting raw product objects into optimized prompt-response pairs for LLM instruction fine-tuning.\n",
    "3. **DAY 3: Evaluation, Baselines & Traditional ML** — Establishing benchmark metrics (MAE, RMSE, Median Error) with dummy baselines, linear regression, and Random Forests.\n",
    "4. **DAY 4: Deep Learning & Frontier LLM Zero-Shot** — Training custom PyTorch neural networks and evaluating zero-shot performance across frontier LLMs (GPT-4o, Claude 3.5 Sonnet, Gemini 1.5/2.0).\n",
    "5. **DAY 5: Fine-tuning an Open-Source LLM** — Using LoRA / QLoRA to specialize an open-source model (Llama 3 / Mistral) to beat general-purpose models on product valuation.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "### 🌐 Data Source\n",
    "We use the academic **Amazon Reviews 2023** dataset curated by the McAuley Lab at UC San Diego:\n",
    "- **Repository**: [McAuley-Lab/Amazon-Reviews-2023](https://huggingface.co/datasets/McAuley-Lab/Amazon-Reviews-2023)\n",
    "- **Metadata Directory**: [raw/meta_categories](https://huggingface.co/datasets/McAuley-Lab/Amazon-Reviews-2023/tree/main/raw/meta_categories)\n",
    "Contains millions of scraped product records across dozens of commercial categories.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "8d710dda",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "<table style=\"margin: 0; text-align: left; border: 1px solid #ddd; border-radius: 8px; padding: 10px;\">\n",
    "    <tr>\n",
    "        <td style=\"width: 140px; vertical-align: middle;\">\n",
    "            <img src=\"../assets/business.jpg\" width=\"130\" style=\"border-radius: 8px; display: block;\" />\n",
    "        </td>\n",
    "        <td style=\"padding-left: 15px;\">\n",
    "            <h3 style=\"color:#1b5e20; margin-top:0;\">💡 The Business Value of Data Curation</h3>\n",
    "            <p style=\"color:#2e7d32; line-height: 1.5; margin-bottom: 0;\">\n",
    "                Data curation is often dismissed as unglamorous, but in real-world ML engineering, <strong>this is where the science actually happens</strong>.\n",
    "                <br/><br/>\n",
    "                As Ed Donner emphasizes from his experiments on this project: <em>\"It was R&amp;D on this dataset that had by far the biggest impact on the quality of results.\"</em> \n",
    "                Curating your data—scrubbing boilerplate HTML, enforcing price bounds, stripping non-semantic IDs, standardizing units, and applying mathematical resampling—consistently outperforms days spent on downstream hyperparameter optimization.\n",
    "            </p>\n",
    "        </td>\n",
    "    </tr>\n",
    "</table>\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "7e75576c",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 1. Environment Setup & Core Dependencies\n",
    "\n",
    "Here we import our foundational libraries:\n",
    "- **`datasets`**: Hugging Face's high-performance library for streaming, manipulating, and caching large tabular/NLP datasets.\n",
    "- **`pricer.items.Item`**: Our custom **Pydantic** data model (`BaseModel`). \n",
    "  > 📌 **Why Pydantic?** Using a Pydantic `BaseModel` gives us strict schema validation, seamless type conversion, and built-in methods to convert to/from JSON. It holds our key fields (`title`, `category`, `price`, `full`, `weight`, `summary`) and provides utility functions to construct LLM training prompts and push datasets directly to the Hugging Face Hub.\n",
    "- **`pricer.parser.parse`**: Domain-specific parsing rules designed to clean raw JSON dictionaries, filter outliers, standardize units, and construct unified text blocks.\n",
    "- **`dotenv`**: Loads API keys and tokens from `.env` without hardcoding sensitive credentials.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "67cedf85-8125-4322-998e-9375fe745597",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# imports\n",
    "\n",
    "import os\n",
    "from dotenv import load_dotenv\n",
    "from huggingface_hub import login\n",
    "from datasets import load_dataset\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "from tqdm.notebook import tqdm\n",
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "import random\n",
    "from pricer.items import Item\n",
    "from pricer.parser import parse\n",
    "load_dotenv(override=True)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "43be730b",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 2. Hugging Face Authentication\n",
    "\n",
    "We authenticate against Hugging Face Hub using the `HF_TOKEN` stored in our `.env` file. This is required for:\n",
    "1. Accessing rate-limited or gated datasets.\n",
    "2. Pushing our final curated datasets (`items_raw_full` and `items_raw_lite`) to your personal Hugging Face profile for downstream tasks.\n",
    "\n",
    "> ℹ️ **Note on Login Output**: If you see a notification stating *'Environment variable HF_TOKEN is set...'*, it can be safely ignored; authentication succeeded. If you run `login()` without arguments, it will interactively prompt you to paste your token.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "0732274a-aa6a-44fc-aee2-40dc8a8e4451",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Log in to HuggingFace - if you get a \"Note\" about Environment variable being set, ignore it\n",
    "\n",
    "hf_token = os.environ['HF_TOKEN']\n",
    "login(hf_token, add_to_git_credential=True)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "7332d5eb",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "---\n",
    "## 3. Initial Exploration: A Single Category (`Appliances`)\n",
    "\n",
    "Before attempting to ingest gigabytes of data across multiple product categories, best practice dictates starting with a single category:\n",
    "- **Fast iterations**: Catch data formatting anomalies, missing keys, and edge cases in seconds rather than hours.\n",
    "- **Schema verification**: Understand how Amazon structures metadata (titles, nested bullet lists, JSON details, and price strings).\n",
    "- **Establish cleansing rules**: Discover what boilerplate or noise exists so we can write robust parsing rules.\n",
    "\n",
    "We start by streaming the full metadata for **Appliances**.\n",
    "\n",
    "> 💡 *Troubleshooting*: If you encounter an error like `\"trust_remote_code is no longer supported\"`, run `!uv add --upgrade datasets==3.6.0` in a new cell, restart your kernel, and re-run.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "049885d4-fdfa-4ff0-a932-4a2ed73928e2",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "dataset = load_dataset(\"McAuley-Lab/Amazon-Reviews-2023\", \"raw_meta_Appliances\", split=\"full\", trust_remote_code=True)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "0e7794f1",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Let's check the total number of records available in the raw Appliances split:\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "cde08860-b393-49b8-a620-06a8c0990a64",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "print(f\"Number of Appliances: {len(dataset):,}\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "3496f528",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 4. Inspecting Raw Datapoints & The \"Missing Price\" Mystery\n",
    "\n",
    "Why are we inspecting `dataset[6]` specifically instead of `dataset[0]`?\n",
    "- If you inspect `dataset[0]`, `dataset[1]`, or `dataset[5]` (e.g. an ice maker machine countertop), you will discover they are **missing prices entirely**!\n",
    "- In web-scraped Amazon data, many items lack prices because they were out of stock, discontinued, or had their price hidden behind a *\"See price in cart\"* button when scraped.\n",
    "- `dataset[6]` happens to be the **very first item** in the dataset with an available, valid price ($8.99 for a rack roller and stud assembly kit).\n",
    "\n",
    "Let's inspect `dataset[6]` to understand the raw dictionary structure:\n",
    "- `price`: Stored as a raw string (`\"8.99\"`).\n",
    "- `features`: List of bullet marketing points.\n",
    "- `description`: Product overview paragraphs.\n",
    "- `details`: Stringified JSON containing technical specifications (weight, dimensions, model numbers).\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "3e29a5ab-ca61-41cc-9b33-22d374681b85",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Investigate a particular datapoint\n",
    "\n",
    "dataset[6]\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "73c16780",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 5. Identifying Price Outliers\n",
    "\n",
    "Web-scraped e-commerce data is notoriously noisy:\n",
    "- Misplaced decimal points (e.g., `$15000.00` for an item that should be `$15.00`).\n",
    "- Industrial/commercial equipment mixed into consumer categories.\n",
    "- Placeholder prices used by third-party sellers (e.g., `$99,999.00`).\n",
    "\n",
    "Let's search for the highest-priced appliance in this dataset to see what extreme values exist:\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "1c3a74d1",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "\n",
    "# What's the most expensive item?\n",
    "\n",
    "max_price = 0\n",
    "max_item = None\n",
    "\n",
    "for datapoint in tqdm(dataset):\n",
    "    try:\n",
    "        price = float(datapoint[\"price\"])\n",
    "        if price > max_price:\n",
    "            max_item = datapoint\n",
    "            max_price = price\n",
    "    except ValueError:\n",
    "        pass\n",
    "\n",
    "print(f\"The most expensive item is {max_item['title']} and it costs {max_price:,.2f}\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "4039d348",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "> 🔍 **Outlier Finding**: The most expensive item found is a **TurboChef Bullet Rapid Cook** electric microwave convection oven priced at **$21,000**!\n",
    ">\n",
    "> When Ed Donner looked this up on Amazon, he found it marked down to ~$15,861 (*\"going at a bargain price, but not an investment I will personally be making!\"*). \n",
    ">\n",
    "> Commercial equipment at extreme price points represents severe distribution outliers that would distort Mean Squared Error (MSE) or Mean Absolute Error (MAE) loss functions during training. For our consumer pricing model, we establish a sensible upper threshold of **$1,000**.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "dc93d3a8",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "---\n",
    "## 6. The Data Cleaning & Standardization Pipeline (`pricer.parser`)\n",
    "\n",
    "To transform noisy raw dictionaries into clean, standardized `Item` objects, our [`pricer/parser.py`](file:///C:/Amit/work/code/AI/llm_engineering/myself/Week6/pricer/parser.py) module enforces the following data curation rules:\n",
    "\n",
    "### 🧹 Cleaning & Quality Filters\n",
    "1. **Valid Numerical Price**:\n",
    "   - Discards entries where `price` cannot be converted to a float (or is missing).\n",
    "   - Restricts prices to **$0.50 ≤ price ≤ $999.49** (`MIN_PRICE` to `MAX_PRICE`). Products under $0.50 are typically accessories or digital glitches; products above $1,000 are commercial outliers.\n",
    "2. **Minimum Information Threshold**:\n",
    "   - Discards products with less than **600 characters** of total text (`MIN_CHARS = 600`). Without sufficient descriptive features, neither human experts nor LLMs can make an accurate valuation.\n",
    "3. **Boilerplate & Metadata Removal**:\n",
    "   - Removes unhelpful Amazon internal fields: `\"Part Number\"`, `\"Best Sellers Rank\"`, `\"Batteries Included?\"`, `\"Batteries Required?\"`, `\"Item model number\"`.\n",
    "4. **Alphanumeric ID / ASIN Stripping**:\n",
    "   - Uses regex `\\b(?=[A-Z0-9]{7,}\\b)(?=.*[A-Z])(?=.*\\d)[A-Z0-9]+\\b` to scrub random alphanumeric serial numbers, UPC codes, and ASINs. These contain zero semantic information and risk causing the model to memorize random strings or consume context tokens needlessly.\n",
    "5. **Length Bounds & Whitespace Simplification**:\n",
    "   - Compresses redundant spaces, newlines, and tabs.\n",
    "   - Caps individual sections at 3,000 characters and the combined description at **4,000 characters** (`MAX_TEXT_TOTAL`).\n",
    "6. **Unit Normalization (`get_weight`)**:\n",
    "   - Standardizes diverse weight units (`ounces`, `grams`, `milligrams`, `kilograms`, `hundredths of pounds`) into a single consistent unit: **pounds**.\n",
    "\n",
    "Let's apply `parse()` across all raw appliance records:\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "40a4e10f-6710-4780-a95e-6c0030c3fb87",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Load into Item objects if they have a price range $1-$1000 and enough details\n",
    "\n",
    "items = [parse(datapoint, \"Appliances\") for datapoint in tqdm(dataset)]\n",
    "items = [item for item in items if item is not None]\n",
    "print(f\"There are {len(items):,} items from {len(dataset):,} datapoints\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "b40173be",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 📊 Filtering Survival Rate & Connecting the Dots:\n",
    "- Out of **94,000 raw appliance datapoints**, only **~35,000 items** survived our parsing rules!\n",
    "- **Why did ~59,000 items get discarded?** Because they lacked prices, had less than 600 characters of description, or were priced outside $0.50–$999.49.\n",
    "- **Notice something interesting about `items[0]`?** It is actually `dataset[6]` from above! Because items 0 through 5 lacked prices and were filtered out, item 6 became our very first valid curated item: a rack roller and stud assembly kit valued at $8.99.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "2fa91b86",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "items[0]"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c43f02e9",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Let's examine the `.full` attribute of `items[0]`. \n",
    "Notice that this combines the title, cleaned description, bullet features, and remaining technical details into a coherent textual document. You may notice occasional small gaps or missing serial codes—that is because our regex in `scrub()` successfully stripped away long alphanumeric part numbers and ASINs!\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "0f9315f4",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "print(items[0].full)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "ebdabacf",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 7. Analyzing Cleansed Distributions (Appliances)\n",
    "\n",
    "Let's extract the list of prices and character lengths to verify the behavior of our parsing pipeline:\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "9d356c6f-b6e8-4e01-98cd-c562d132aafa",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "prices = [item.price for item in items]\n",
    "lengths = [len(item.full) for item in items]"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "b22a1dac",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 📏 Text Length Analysis & The 4,000 Character Spike:\n",
    "- **Average length**: ~1,400 characters (approximately 300–350 LLM tokens).\n",
    "- **The Visible Spike at 4,000 characters**: Notice the distinct spike at the right edge of the histogram. That spike exists because our parser deliberately truncated all descriptions longer than 4,000 characters (`MAX_TEXT_TOTAL`).\n",
    "\n",
    "**Why this matters for LLM Engineering**:\n",
    "1. **Cutting the \"Waffle\"**: E-commerce descriptions often waffle on with endless repetitive marketing copy. Truncating at 4,000 characters retains all essential product details while discarding fluff.\n",
    "2. **Context Window Efficiency**: Descriptions under 4,000 characters guarantee that complete prompt-response pairs fit comfortably within 1,024 context tokens during fine-tuning.\n",
    "3. **GPU Memory & Cost**: Attention mechanism complexity scales with sequence length ($O(N^2)$ or $O(N)$ with FlashAttention). Capping sequence length prevents Out-Of-Memory (OOM) errors and drastically reduces training time.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "89078cb1-9679-4eb0-b295-599b8586bcd1",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Plot the distribution of lengths\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.figure(figsize=(15, 6))\n",
    "plt.title(f\"Lengths: Avg {sum(lengths)/len(lengths):,.0f} and highest {max(lengths):,}\\n\")\n",
    "plt.xlabel('Length (chars)')\n",
    "plt.ylabel('Count')\n",
    "plt.hist(lengths, rwidth=0.7, color=\"lightblue\", bins=range(0, 6000, 100))\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "1c6a616b",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Let's inspect the item that reached the maximum length of 4,000 characters (a polyester dryer vent filter replacement part).\n",
    "Notice how much repetitive marketing text it contains (*\"remember to clean out your lint filter between each load...\"*). This illustrates why capping at 4,000 characters is essential—we capture the item's specifications without letting endless seller waffle overwhelm our context budget.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "295be519",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "max_length = max(lengths)\n",
    "max_length_item = items[lengths.index(max_length)]\n",
    "print(max_length_item.full)\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "745073da",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 💰 Price Distribution Analysis (Appliances)\n",
    "\n",
    "Let's plot a histogram of prices for the cleaned Appliances dataset. Notice the **extreme right-skew (exponential decay)**:\n",
    "- The average price is ~$56, but the vast majority of items are clustered under $50 (with a massive peak around $20–$30).\n",
    "- A very thin long tail extends out to $999.49.\n",
    "- **Modeling Challenge**: If trained directly on this distribution, any machine learning model will gravitate toward predicting $20–$30 for every item to minimize mean error, failing completely on higher-value products. We will solve this later with **weighted resampling**!\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "c38e0c43-9f7a-450e-a911-c94d37d9b9c3",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Plot the distribution of prices\n",
    "plt.figure(figsize=(15, 6))\n",
    "plt.title(f\"Prices: Avg {sum(prices)/len(prices):,.2f} and highest {max(prices):,}\\n\")\n",
    "plt.xlabel('Price ($)')\n",
    "plt.ylabel('Count')\n",
    "plt.hist(prices, rwidth=0.7, color=\"orange\", bins=range(0, 1000, 10))\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "44803113",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Let's inspect another sample item (`items[3]`, an MLB stainless steel dishwasher magnet cover) to verify formatting, materials, and dimension metadata across different appliance product types:\n"
   ]
  },
