   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "71986fef",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "---\n",
    "## 9. Deduplication Strategy: Avoiding the \"False Validation Trap\"\n",
    "\n",
    "In e-commerce datasets, duplicate items are rampant:\n",
    "- The same product is often listed under multiple categories (e.g. an OBD-II scanner listed under both *Automotive* and *Electronics*).\n",
    "- Multiple third-party sellers copy and paste the identical product description and title across different ASINs.\n",
    "\n",
    "### 🚨 Why Duplicates are Insidious: Dataset Leakage & The False Validation Trap\n",
    "As Ed Donner highlights, dataset contamination is one of the most dangerous pitfalls in machine learning:\n",
    "1. **Data Leakage**: If identical items land in both the training set and the validation/test set, your evaluation does not measure true generalization—it measures **rote memorization**.\n",
    "2. **The \"False Validation Trap\"**: Because the model memorizes leaked duplicate items, validation loss keeps decreasing and validation accuracy looks deceptively high. You assume the model is learning generalized patterns and continue training, when in reality it is severely **overfitting**. When evaluated on genuinely unseen products in production, performance collapses!\n",
    "\n",
    "### ⚙️ The \"Slightly Cunning\" Two-Stage Deduplication Code:\n",
    "1. `random.shuffle(items)`: Randomize order to avoid chronological or category bias.\n",
    "2. **Set-Based Deduplication Idiom**:\n",
    "   ```python\n",
    "   seen = set()\n",
    "   items = [x for x in tqdm(items) if not (x.title in seen or seen.add(x.title))]\n",
    "   ```\n",
    "   *How it works*: In Python, `seen.add(x)` returns `None` (falsy). If `x.title` is not in `seen`, `x.title in seen` is `False`, the `or` condition evaluates `seen.add()`, adds the title to the set, and evaluates to `False`, so `not False` keeps the item! This deduplicates millions of items in a single blazing-fast pass.\n",
    "3. **Stage 1 (Title Deduplication)** followed by **Stage 2 (Full Text Deduplication)**:\n",
    "   Removes over **100,000 duplicate items**, bringing our dataset down from ~2.98 million to **2,887,000 unique items**.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "c25da1e5",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "random.seed(42)\n",
    "random.shuffle(items)\n",
    "\n",
    "seen = set()\n",
    "items = [x for x in tqdm(items) if not (x.title in seen or seen.add(x.title))]\n",
    "\n",
    "seen = set()\n",
    "items = [x for x in tqdm(items) if not (x.full in seen or seen.add(x.full))]\n",
    "\n",
    "del seen\n",
    "print(f\"After deduplication, we have {len(items):,} items\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "9230355a",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 10. Diagnostics on the Deduplicated Multi-Category Dataset\n",
