   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "d7c55b75",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "---\n",
    "## 12. Stratified & Weighted Resampling: The \"Dark Art\" of Dataset Engineering\n",
    "\n",
    "To extract our target sample of **820,000 items** from the larger population, naive random selection would reproduce two severe flaws:\n",
    "1. **Low-Price Dominance**: 80%+ of products cost under $30, leaving the model blind to mid- and high-priced goods.\n",
    "2. **Automotive Over-Representation**: Over 1 million automotive products dominating the dataset.\n",
    "\n",
    "### 📐 The Mathematical Resampling Strategy:\n",
    "```python\n",
    "# 1. Normalize prices to range [0, 1]\n",
    "p = (prices - prices.min()) / (prices.max() - prices.min() + 1e-9)\n",
    "\n",
    "# 2. Quadratic price weighting: Favors more expensive items\n",
    "w = p**2\n",
    "\n",
    "# 3. Category penalties: Down-weights over-represented categories\n",
    "w[categories == \"Tools_and_Home_Improvement\"] *= 0.5\n",
    "w[categories == \"Automotive\"] *= 0.05\n",
    "\n",
    "# 4. Normalize weights into a valid probability distribution (sums to 1.0)\n",
    "w = w / w.sum()\n",
    "\n",
    "# 5. Sample exactly 820,000 items without replacement according to probability vector w\n",
    "idx = np.random.choice(len(items), size=SIZE, replace=False, p=w)\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "### 💡 Crucial Insights on the Resampling Formula:\n",
    "- **Why $w = p^2$?** Squaring the normalized price acts as an aggressive soft high-pass filter. An item costing $500 receives exponentially higher probability of selection than a $10 item, counter-balancing the extreme scarcity of expensive goods.\n",
    "- **Why did Automotive require such a severe penalty (`*= 0.05`)?**\n",
    "  As Ed Donner discovered during experimentation: *car parts frequently happen to be among the more expensive items*. When we applied quadratic price weighting ($w = p^2$), it inadvertently boosted expensive car parts even further! A moderate penalty wasn't enough—he had to apply a massive 95% penalty (`*= 0.05`) to prevent Automotive from swamping the entire dataset.\n",
    "- **The \"Dark Art\" vs. Scientific Experimentation**:\n",
    "  Tuning weights can feel heuristic or hand-wavy. As Ed emphasizes: *\"The scientific way is to curate multiple dataset candidates with different weighting schemes, train models on each, and see which performs best on your business outcome metric.\"* This specific formulation was empirically proven through multiple training runs to deliver the best valuation accuracy.\n",
    "- **Why `random.seed(42)`?**\n",
    "  1. **Proof of No Cherry-Picking**: Seed 42 is the universal convention in data science. It demonstrates that you didn't test seeds 1 through 100 to artificially inflate your evaluation metrics.\n",
    "  2. **The Hitchhiker's Guide to the Galaxy**: A famous nerdy homage to Douglas Adams' answer to *\"the ultimate question of life, the universe, and everything.\"*\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "d70e9d40",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "np.random.seed(42)\n",
    "\n",
    "SIZE = 820_000\n",
    "\n",
    "prices = np.array([it.price for it in items], dtype=float)\n",
    "categories = np.array([it.category for it in items])\n",
    "p = (prices - prices.min()) / (prices.max() - prices.min() + 1e-9)\n",
    "\n",
    "w = p**2\n",
    "w[categories == \"Tools_and_Home_Improvement\"] *= 0.5\n",
    "w[categories == \"Automotive\"] *= 0.05\n",
    "\n",
    "w = w / w.sum()\n",
    "idx = np.random.choice(len(items), size=SIZE, replace=False, p=w)\n",
    "sample = [items[i] for i in idx]\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "558dabda",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 13. Validating the Curated Sample: The Shift in Distribution\n",
    "\n",
    "Let's examine the price histogram of our resampled 820,000 items:\n",
    "\n",
    "### 📊 Key Changes to the Distribution:\n",
    "1. **Average Price Shift**: In the raw dataset, the average price was **~$59**. In this resampled dataset, the average price jumps to **~$140**—a far more realistic and balanced reflection of consumer products across diverse categories.\n",
    "2. **Fatter Right Tail**: Notice the much thicker distribution between $100 and $800, providing ample training examples for mid-to-high ticket items.\n",
    "3. **The Spikes at $199, $299, $399, $499**:\n",
    "   You will notice distinct periodic spikes across the histogram. \n",
    "   - *Why are there spikes?* These correspond to **psychological retail pricing** ($99 price endings like $199, $299, $499).\n",
    "   - *Why don't we smooth them out?* Because this is a **real-world feature of e-commerce data**, not an artifact! We explicitly want our LLM to learn and reflect authentic retail pricing patterns.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "b695692b",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "prices = [item.price for item in sample]\n",
    "plt.figure(figsize=(15, 6))\n",
